Subject: [oss-security] Reminder: QtWebKit known vulnerabilities

Quoting Christophe Giboudeaux [0] (excerpted, rewrapped),

> QtWebKit was a rendering engine for web content released with Qt until=20
> 5.6. It was replaced with QtWebEngine after that.
>=20
> Despite a community fork in 2016, nothing really happened to keep it=20
> alive and secure.
> Quoting the QtWebKit release page on Github:
>=20
> "WARNING: This release is based on old WebKit revision with known=20
> unpatched vulnerabilities.
> Please use it carefully and avoid visiting untrusted websites and=20
> using it for transmission of sensitive data."

As of today, Repology reports that 246 packages still exist for=20
qt5-webkit, in dozens of Linux distros (most of them overcounted, e.g.=20
qt5-qtwebkit-dev).

Of particular concern is the 104 packages known for wkhtmltopdf. As=20
repeated multiple times on the website (unfortunately not prominently=20
enough), "Do not use wkhtmltopdf with any untrusted HTML". However, it's=20
fair to say that many users likely do not follow this advice.

Readers of this list will likely be familiar with the regular postings=20
regarding WebKitGTK vulnerabilities: many of them are likely applicable=20
to QtWebKit too, especially the WebKitGTK-based fork.

Fortunately, as opposed to the webkitgtk issues a few years ago, most=20
programs now support Qt WebEngine, QTextBrowser (part of Qt Widgets), or=20
don't require any browser engine anymore. Almost all exceptions are no=20
longer maintained upstream. wkhtmltopdf is likely the most used package=20
that still requires QtWebKit, but in my opinion is the most dangerous,=20
as users may unknowingly install it from distro repositories and use it=20
to process untrusted data. Users can migrate to weasyprint, puppeteer,=20
or another alternative.

Alpine Linux has removed QtWebKit [1], Gentoo Linux has masked it [2]=20
(meaning users must manually approve the installation), and OpenSUSE=20
will be removing it soon [0]. In the absence of a maintained QtWebKit,=20
I urge other distros to follow suit to protect Linux users.

[0] https://lists.opensuse.org/archives/list/factory@lists.opensuse.org/thr=
